  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png)\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-3,$ while $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities.",
